  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 511 AM MDT Fri Aug 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nStrong ridging aloft will remain in place today before a trough \nmoving onto the Pacific NW coast brings a more unstable \nsouthwesterly flow to the Northern Rockies tonight into Saturday. \nVery warm and dry conditions generally prevail through the weekend\neven while the ridge breakdown process takes place. Most lower \nelevation locations will see afternoon highs well into the 80s to \nthe lower 90s. Subtle shortwaves will begin moving through and \ninteracting with increasing instability this evening into tonight.\nThis will send a wave of widely scattered high based showers and \nthunderstorms moving in a southwest to northeast fashion this \nevening into tonight with the primary impact being localized gusty\nwinds and lightning, especially for central and southwest MT. \n\nStronger shortwaves look to send a more impactful wave showers \nand thunderstorms through the forecast area late Saturday \nafternoon into Saturday night. ML CAPE doesn't look exceptionally \nhigh in the 400 to 900 J/kg range, but forcing from the shortwave \nwill make up some of the void and very warm and dry sub cloud \nlayers will increase instances of strong gusty winds up to and \nexceeding 60 mph, especially if linear upscale growth takes place.\nHail and brief downpours downpours capable of flash flooding of \nnormally flood prone areas will also be present with bulk shear \ngenerally above 25 kts and PWATS approaching and even surpassing \nthe one inch mark in some cases. The focus of this activity will \nbe over southwest MT and and along the Continental Divide, though \nat least some of it will move northeastward onto the plains during\nthe overnight hours.\n\nThe Pacific NW trough will swing northeastward through W MT and \ninto Canada on Sunday while its attendant surface front traverses \nthe CWA and brings gusty westerly winds and elevated fire weather \nconditions. There will also be lingering instability for widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms, some on the strong side, \nmainly for locations east of I15. Temperatures modestly cooldown \nMonday and Tuesday with some shower and thunderstorm activity in \nsouthwest MT. This looks to be followed by ridging aloft briefly \nrebuilding before another Pacific trough approaches during the mid\nto latter part of next week. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nScattered shower and thunderstorm activity this evening through \nSunday, strongest and most widespread Saturday...\n\nAn unstable southwesterly flow aloft will send multiple waves of \nshowers and thunderstorms through the forecast areas this evening \nthrough Sunday. The initial wave will move into southwest MT by \nthis evening before moving northeastward during the overnight \nhours. This activity will be isolated in nature and mostly high \nbased with little precipitation. The primary concern will be \nlocalized gusty winds over 30 mph and lightning, mostly for \nsouthwestern locations.\n\nThe stronger shortwave energy arrives Saturday afternoon into \nSaturday night. Strong gusty winds in excess of 60 mph and \nlightning will be the primary hazards on Saturday, especially if \nthe storms become linear and undergo upscale growth. This threat \nwill be highest over southwest MT and along the Continental \nDivide. Some weakening is expected with this activity as it moves \nnortheastward, though these same hazards apply to plains locations\nas well. In addition to the winds, there will be localized heavy \ndownpours capable of flash flooding of burn scars and other flood \nprone locations, mainly in southwest MT where there is around a \n10% chance for excessive rainfall and localized flooding. The \nfocus of this activity shifts eastward on Sunday with a few \nstronger storms for locations east of I15. Central/north-central \nlocations east and southeast of the highway 87 corridor will see \nthe strongest storm ceiling with strong, gusty winds, hail, and \ndownpours all on the table. - RCG",
